    <div class="prose">
      <p>Canada's nonstop flight landscape is dominated by Toronto Pearson, which operates more international direct routes than the other three major airports combined. But Vancouver, Montreal, and Calgary each have meaningful nonstop coverage — particularly for their respective geographic strengths. This guide covers the key routes from each airport, which are year-round, which are seasonal, and which destinations are connection-only no matter which airline you choose.</p>
      <h2>Toronto Pearson (YYZ) — Canada's Busiest Hub</h2>
      <p>Toronto Pearson is by far Canada's most connected airport for international nonstop travel. Air Canada, WestJet, and a large number of foreign carriers all serve YYZ directly, giving Torontonians unmatched flight options without a connection.</p>
      <h3>Year-Round Nonstop from YYZ</h3>
      <p>London Heathrow (LHR) is served daily by Air Canada, British Airways, and WestJet (to Gatwick). Paris CDG receives Air Canada and Air France daily. Frankfurt has daily Air Canada service. Amsterdam, Dublin, Lisbon, and Zurich all offer year-round nonstop connections from Pearson. In the Americas, New York (JFK, EWR, LGA), Los Angeles, Miami, Chicago, San Francisco, and dozens of US cities have nonstop service. Cancun and Nassau are year-round leisure routes. Tokyo Narita and Haneda both receive Air Canada nonstop service. Hong Kong has Air Canada daily. Delhi, Mumbai, and Bangalore have year-round nonstop on Air Canada and Air India.</p>
      <h3>Seasonal Nonstop from YYZ</h3>
      <p>Rome, Barcelona, Madrid, Athens, and Edinburgh get nonstop service in summer (May through October) but require connections in winter. Seasonal Caribbean routes expand significantly December through April, covering Barbados, St. Lucia, Jamaica, Cuba, and Turks and Caicos. Maui and Honolulu see more frequent nonstop in winter. Budapest, Prague, and Warsaw get seasonal summer nonstop from Air Transat and charter operators.</p>
      <div class="info-box"><p>ℹ️ Air Transat specializes in seasonal transatlantic nonstop from Canada. They add significant summer capacity to European leisure destinations that lack year-round Air Canada service — often at meaningfully lower prices.</p></div>
      <h2>Vancouver International (YVR) — Pacific Gateway</h2>
      <p>Vancouver's position on Canada's west coast makes it the natural Canadian gateway for Asia-Pacific routes. YVR has stronger trans-Pacific nonstop coverage than any other Canadian airport, and travelers heading to East Asia or the South Pacific are better served from Vancouver than from any other Canadian city.</p>
      <h3>Year-Round Nonstop from YVR</h3>
      <p>Tokyo Narita and Haneda receive Air Canada daily nonstop. Seoul Incheon is served by Air Canada and Korean Air. Hong Kong gets daily Air Canada service. Sydney, Australia has Air Canada nonstop — one of the more impressive ultra-long-haul routes from North America. Shanghai Pudong and Beijing have nonstop from Air Canada and Chinese carriers. Manila gets Philippine Airlines nonstop. London Heathrow is served by Air Canada daily. Auckland, New Zealand has Air Canada nonstop year-round. Los Angeles, San Francisco, Seattle, and Las Vegas are high-frequency domestic and trans-border routes.</p>
      <h3>Seasonal Nonstop from YVR</h3>
      <p>Osaka Kansai, Nagoya, and Sapporo get seasonal Air Canada summer service. Paris CDG and Frankfurt open seasonal summer routes. Mexican resort cities including Los Cabos, Puerto Vallarta, and Cancun ramp up significantly in winter months.</p>
      <div class="tip-box"><p>💡 If you are traveling from Vancouver to Southeast Asia — Bangkok, Singapore, Bali, Ho Chi Minh City — you will likely need a connection. Air Canada's Pacific routing goes through Tokyo or Hong Kong rather than directly into Southeast Asia from YVR. Check Singapore Airlines or Thai Airways for one-stop options to this region.</p></div>
      <h2>Montreal Trudeau (YUL) — Transatlantic Strength</h2>
      <p>Montreal has solid transatlantic nonstop coverage, particularly to France and French-speaking Europe, thanks to strong cultural and business ties between Quebec and France. Domestic and US trans-border options are comprehensive, and several North African routes serve Montreal's substantial diaspora communities directly.</p>
      <h3>Year-Round Nonstop from YUL</h3>
      <p>Paris CDG is served by Air Canada, Air France, and Air Transat — making it one of the most frequently served transatlantic routes from Montreal, with several departures daily. Casablanca and Algiers have year-round nonstop from Royal Air Maroc and Air Algerie. London Heathrow gets Air Canada daily. New York (JFK and EWR), Miami, and Orlando are year-round trans-border routes. Zurich receives Lufthansa Group nonstop service. Several Francophone African destinations have nonstop connections due to Montreal's position as a hub for French-speaking travelers.</p>
      <h3>Seasonal Nonstop from YUL</h3>
      <p>Lisbon, Barcelona, Athens, Rome, and Lyon get summer nonstop service primarily from Air Transat. Haiti (Port-au-Prince) gets additional frequency during holiday periods. Guadeloupe and Martinique have strong nonstop service December through April for Caribbean sun travelers.</p>
      <h3>Connections Required from YUL</h3>
      <p>Most Asia-Pacific destinations require a connection via Toronto or a European hub from Montreal. There is no nonstop service from YUL to any Asian city. For Australasia, South America beyond a handful of routes, and most of sub-Saharan Africa, connections are required regardless of which airline you choose.</p>
      <h2>Calgary International (YYC) — Western Canada's Hub</h2>
      <p>Calgary International has fewer nonstop routes than the other three major airports but punches above its weight for transatlantic connections — particularly to the UK — and has comprehensive coverage of US trans-border routes and leisure destinations.</p>
      <h3>Year-Round Nonstop from YYC</h3>
      <p>London Heathrow gets WestJet and Air Canada nonstop. Las Vegas, Los Angeles, San Francisco, Denver, Phoenix, Chicago, Dallas, and most major US cities have year-round nonstop from Calgary. Cancun is a high-frequency year-round leisure route. Toronto and Vancouver are domestic trunk routes with dozens of daily departures serving the country's busiest corridors.</p>
      <h3>Seasonal Nonstop from YYC</h3>
      <p>Amsterdam gets Air Canada seasonal service in summer. Dublin and Glasgow receive WestJet seasonal nonstop in summer — WestJet has been particularly strong for direct Calgary-to-UK routes that bypass Toronto. Caribbean resort cities ramp up significantly in winter including Varadero, Montego Bay, and Puerto Plata.</p>
      <h3>Connections Required from YYC</h3>
      <p>Asia, the Middle East, South America, Africa, and most of southern Europe require connections from Calgary — typically through Toronto, Vancouver, or a European hub. For travelers connecting onward to Europe's secondary cities, Amsterdam and Frankfurt are both well-suited given the existing direct connections from YYC.</p>
      <div class="tip-box"><p>💡 Canadian travelers flying to niche European destinations from Calgary or Montreal often get better results connecting through Amsterdam Schiphol or Frankfurt than through London Heathrow, which can be more congested and have tighter connection windows during peak periods.</p></div>
      <h2>Airlines Operating Nonstop from Canada</h2>
      <p>Air Canada is the dominant carrier for long-haul nonstop from all four airports, with the largest network of direct international routes. Their Star Alliance membership allows mileage redemption through United MileagePlus, Lufthansa Miles and More, and their own Aeroplan program. WestJet has grown its transatlantic nonstop significantly in recent years, particularly on UK routes from Calgary and Toronto, and their WestJet Business cabin offers a competitive product at lower prices than Air Canada on many routes. Air Transat specializes in seasonal leisure routes to Europe and the Caribbean and is often the cheapest nonstop option on summer transatlantic routes. Foreign carriers including Air France, British Airways, Lufthansa, KLM, Emirates, and several Asian carriers all add nonstop capacity from YYZ and to a lesser extent YVR, giving travelers additional choices and redemption options across non-Canadian loyalty programs.</p>
      <h2>When Connections Are Unavoidable from Canada</h2>
      <p>Some destinations simply have no nonstop service from Canada. Most of Southeast Asia requires a connection — there are no nonstop flights from any Canadian city to Bangkok, Bali, Singapore from most airports, or Ho Chi Minh City. Most of South America is connection-only. Sub-Saharan Africa, Central Asia, and Oceania beyond Sydney and Auckland all require at least one stop. When a connection is required, Air Canada's Toronto hub and WestJet's Calgary hub are the two most natural Canadian connecting points for North American travelers. For transatlantic connections, Amsterdam, Frankfurt, and London all offer good onward networks and historically reliable operations.</p>
